	affiliations = {Hajee Mohammad Danesh Science and Technology University, Department of Mechanical Engineering, Dinajpur, Bangladesh; Rajshahi University of Engineering and Technology, Department of Mechanical Engineering, Rajshahi, Bangladesh},
	abstract = {The rapid industrialization and urbanization of Bangladesh have led to the deposition of various waste streams, such as textile wastes, cigarette butts, poultry wastes, and fish wastes, which contribute significantly to environmental and public health issues. In the current work, the sustainable production of renewable hydrogen from available local wastes through Aspen Plus simulation is explored, which is the twin benefit of clean energy production as well as safe disposal of wastes. Apart from hydrogen production, the research also emphasizes conversion of toxic by-products into valuable co-products such as calcium carbonate (CaCO<inf>3</inf>), nitrogen, and sulfur, thereby reducing environmental hazard and yielding economically useful materials. The results show that hydrogen yields ranging from 50 to 70 g/kg of dry waste were found at purity levels > 95 %, with the cost of levelized hydrogen ($1.8–2.5/kg). Also, the Sensitivity analyses were conducted to determine the optimal operational parameters, indicating that elevated temperatures and reduced pressures the maximization of hydrogen selectivity. At the same time, multi-variable optimization revealed feedstock synergies that improved yields by 10 % to 20 %. Simulation results demonstrate that valorization of wastes combined with hydrogen production can be an effective path towards stopping waste accumulation, promoting green energy adoption, and enabling a circular economy for Bangladesh. This study focuses on a novel approach to harnessing the energy potential of industrial and urban wastes for the safe and sustainable utilization of toxic residues. © 2025 The Author(s)},
